# Claude Code Rules for Data School Readiness Engine
# This file is the "Permanent System Prompt" for all AI-assisted development

## CRITICAL: Read These Documents First
Before making ANY changes to the codebase:
1. Read `docs/PRD_DataSRE.md` — Product vision and features
2. Read `docs/TRD_DataSRE.md` — Technical architecture
3. Read `docs/SRD_DataSRE.md` — Safety requirements
4. Read `docs/DATA_ANALYST_CONTEXT.md` — Existing codebase history

## Tech Stack Constraints (DO NOT DEVIATE)
- **Frontend:** Static HTML + React 18 (CDN) + Tailwind CSS (CDN)
  - NO build step, NO webpack, NO vite, NO npm scripts for frontend
  - NO TypeScript (not in MVP scope)
  - All React code must work with Babel transpiler in-browser
- **Backend:** Vercel Serverless Functions (Node.js 20+)
  - CommonJS (`require`) for now, ESM migration is Phase 0
- **Database:** Supabase PostgreSQL (NOT SQLite after Phase 0)
- **AI Provider:** Google Gemini (free tier) with Anthropic Claude fallback
  - ALWAYS use `api/lib/llm.js` adapter, NEVER call providers directly
- **Hosting:** Vercel (free tier)

## Architectural Integrity Rules
1. **Adapter Pattern Required:** All LLM calls go through `api/lib/llm.js`
2. **No Direct Provider Calls:** Never import `@anthropic-ai/sdk` or `@google/generative-ai` in feature code
3. **Stateless Functions:** All serverless functions are stateless; state lives in Supabase
4. **No Build Tools:** Frontend must work by opening `index.html` directly
5. **Single Entry Point:** All UI lives in one `index.html` file

## Code Change Guidelines
- **Minimize Changes:** Only modify code directly related to the current task
- **No Refactoring Sprees:** Don't "clean up" unrelated code
- **Preserve Comments:** Keep all "TEACHING MOMENT" comments
- **No Secret Commits:** Never hardcode API keys; use `process.env.VAR_NAME`
- **Graceful Degradation:** Errors should degrade gracefully, never crash the app

## Security Mandates (From SRD)
- **IP Hashing:** Always use SHA-256, never store raw IPs
- **Consent Checks:** Data only saved when `consentGiven === true`
- **Prompt Injection Guards:** All LLM system prompts must include guard blocks
- **Input Validation:** Validate all user input before database writes
- **Rate Limiting:** All endpoints that call LLMs or write to DB must be rate limited
- **CORS Restriction:** Never use `Access-Control-Allow-Origin: *` in production

## Privacy (Five C's Framework)
- **Consent:** Users must opt-in to data collection
- **Collection Limits:** Never collect PII (no names, emails, phone numbers)
- **Confidentiality:** IP addresses hashed with SHA-256
- **Control:** Users can opt out without losing functionality
- **Communication:** Privacy policy explains all practices in plain language

## Development Workflow
1. **Plan Before Coding:** Show proposed changes, get approval from Josue
2. **One Feature at a Time:** Complete vertical slices, not scattered partials
3. **Test Incrementally:** Verify each change works before moving on
4. **Commit Frequently:** Commit after each working feature
5. **Update Docs:** When decisions are made, update the relevant document

## File Naming and Organization
- API routes: `/api/[feature-name].js`
- Shared libs: `/api/lib/[module-name].js`
- Datasets: `/data/sprint-[n]-[description].csv`
- Docs: `/docs/[PURPOSE]_[TOPIC].md`
- Scripts: `/scripts/[action]-[noun].js` (e.g., `generate-dataset.js`)

## Testing Patterns
- **Manual First:** Use curl/fetch to test API routes before UI integration
- **Browser Console:** Check for errors in browser console after every change
- **Database Verification:** Query Supabase dashboard to verify data writes
- **End-to-End Smoke Test:** Complete user flow must work before marking phase done

## When to Push Back
If Josue asks for something that conflicts with:
- The SRD security requirements → Explain the risk, suggest alternatives
- The TRD architecture constraints → Point to the constraint, propose workaround
- The "No Build Step" principle → Remind about deployment simplicity goal
- The Five C's privacy framework → Explain privacy violation, offer fix

## Communication Style with Josue
- **Non-Technical Explanations:** Josue is a "vibecoder," not a CS grad
- **Show, Don't Assume:** Explain WHY you're doing something, not just WHAT
- **Teaching Moments:** Add comments explaining patterns for future reference
- **One Step at a Time:** Break complex tasks into small, verifiable chunks
- **Visual Progress:** Show diffs, examples, and before/after comparisons

## Red Flags (STOP and Ask First)
- Adding new npm dependencies → Check if really needed, propose alternatives
- Changing database schema → Confirm migration strategy exists
- Modifying existing API routes → Verify backward compatibility
- Introducing new providers (auth, storage, etc.) → Verify free tier limits
- Suggesting "we should rebuild this in [framework]" → This violates the no-build-step constraint

## Phase-Specific Constraints
Refer to `docs/TRD_DataSRE.md` Section 10 for phase-specific implementation order.
- **Phase 0:** Foundation fixes only (rate limiting, Supabase migration, error boundaries)
- **Phase 1:** Assessment expansion (Phase B diagnostic)
- **Phase 2:** Sprint system (Sprints 1-2 only)
- **Phase 3:** Portfolio generation
- **Phase 4:** Remaining sprints (3-6)
- **Phase 5:** Polish and launch

Do NOT implement features from future phases. Stay in sequence.

## Remember
- This is a **learning project** — prioritize Josue's education over optimal code
- This is a **portfolio piece** — prioritize working demos over perfect architecture
- This is a **zero-cost operation** — prioritize free tiers over paid services
- This is a **solo project** — prioritize simplicity over scalability

When in doubt, ask Josue. Don't make assumptions.

## Accessibility Requirements (Section 508 / WCAG 2.0 AA)

All web content must conform to the Revised Section 508 Standards (WCAG 2.0 Level AA).
Apply these rules to every component, page, and interaction built.

### 1. Perceivable
- Every non-text element (images, icons, charts) must have descriptive `alt` text. Decorative images use `alt=""`.
- Color contrast ratio: **4.5:1 minimum** for normal text, **3:1 for large text**.
- Never use color alone to convey meaning — always pair with text, icons, or patterns.
- Text must be resizable up to 200% without loss of content or functionality.

### 2. Operable
- Every interactive element (buttons, links, inputs, modals) must be fully keyboard navigable — no mouse required.
- **Visible focus indicators required** on all focusable elements. Never remove the default outline without a clearly visible replacement.
- No content should flash more than 3 times per second.
- Page titles must be descriptive and unique per page/view.

### 3. Understandable
- Use plain language appropriate for a general audience.
- All form inputs must have visible, associated `<label>` elements — never placeholder-only labels.
- Error messages must identify the field and explain how to fix it. Use `aria-describedby` to connect errors to inputs.
- Consistent navigation and layout across all pages/screens.

### 4. Robust (Assistive Technology Compatible)
- Use semantic HTML: `<nav>`, `<main>`, `<header>`, `<footer>`, `<section>`, `<h1>`–`<h6>` in logical order.
- Only one `<h1>` per page. Heading levels must not skip (e.g., h1 → h3 is not allowed).
- All interactive components use proper WAI-ARIA roles, states, and properties where native HTML is insufficient.
- Dynamic content updates (AI feedback loading, errors) must use `aria-live` regions.

### 5. React / JavaScript Specific
- Manage focus explicitly when modals open/close or views change.
- Use `role="alert"` or `aria-live="polite"` for dynamic status messages.
- Avoid `onClick` on non-interactive elements — use `<button>` or `<a>` instead.

### 6. Forms & Assessments (Critical for this project)
- Every input in the assessment must have a visible label.
- Required fields must be marked with `aria-required="true"` AND a visible indicator.
- Inline validation errors must be announced via `aria-live`.

### Quick Contrast Reference (Tailwind on white background)
- `text-gray-400` (#9ca3af) → **2.6:1 — FAILS** (never use for text)
- `text-gray-500` (#6b7280) → **4.6:1 — passes AA**
- `text-gray-600` (#4b5563) → **7.0:1 — passes AA and AAA**
- Use `text-gray-600` as the minimum for any body/label text on white.
